WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.140
Where is the file that maps everything
we run, and who can read it?

00:00:04.375 --> 00:00:06.125
It is called the Terraform state.

00:00:06.485 --> 00:00:10.015
Five things decide whether it
is an asset or a liability.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:13.060
First, what state is.

00:00:13.385 --> 00:00:14.895
The code says what we want.

00:00:15.205 --> 00:00:17.715
The state records what
was built, and its ID.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:20.050
The cloud is what actually runs.

00:00:20.405 --> 00:00:23.275
Lose the state,
and the tool forgets what it owns.

00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:25.493
Second, secrets.

00:00:25.933 --> 00:00:31.423
The tool's documentation says a secret in
the configuration is stored in the state.

00:00:31.708 --> 00:00:33.678
Cloud storage encrypts it at rest,

00:00:34.108 --> 00:00:36.638
but decrypts it for
everyone with read access.

00:00:37.063 --> 00:00:40.273
So the reader list matters more
than the encryption setting.

00:00:41.283 --> 00:00:43.373
Third, two writers, one file.

00:00:43.653 --> 00:00:47.723
If a pipeline and an engineer change
the same infrastructure at once,

00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:49.523
one overwrites the other.

00:00:49.758 --> 00:00:51.108
Locking stops that.

00:00:51.393 --> 00:00:55.323
With Amazon S3,
it is switched off unless you turn it on.

00:00:55.608 --> 00:00:59.418
Versioning keeps earlier copies,
so a bad change can be undone.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.140
Fourth, who builds the
place where state lives?

00:01:03.505 --> 00:01:06.875
The storage must exist
before Terraform can use it.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.740
A usual way out is to start with a
small local setup, build the locked,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:15.430
versioned, encrypted bucket,
and then move the state in.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:17.920
Fifth, where the state lives.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.650
HCP Terraform, GitLab,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.270
Amazon S3 and Azure storage
all encrypt at rest.

00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:26.585
The difference is who can read it.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:28.760
What good looks like.

00:01:29.045 --> 00:01:31.755
Remote state, never in a code repository.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.960
A short, reviewed reader list.

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:35.800
No secrets in the state.

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:36.980
Locking on.

00:01:37.325 --> 00:01:38.295
Versions on.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:40.880
A documented bootstrap, with an owner.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:43.660
NIS2 Article 21 lists backup,

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.220
access control, asset management.

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.400
Well run state can support them.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:51.340
A suggested first step.

00:01:51.625 --> 00:01:52.975
Ask for the list of readers,

00:01:53.365 --> 00:01:54.755
not the encryption setting.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.710
And ask whether anyone
has tried restoring it.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.000
Treat the state as a crown jewel.

00:02:01.345 --> 00:02:02.195
Give it an owner.

